    "evidence_text": "Also, translation into English has higher scores than into Estonian/Latvian, which is also expected.\n\nAn interesting side-effect we have observed is the model's resilience to code-switching in the input text.\n\nSee more examples in Appendix SECREF8 .\n\nThat only\n\nmeans that for GEC we have to select an output domain/style when producing error corrections.\n\nGLEU score  for the other corpora.\n\nResults\tThe M INLINEFORM0 scores are computed based on error-annotated corpora.\n\nOur model gets the M INLINEFORM0 score of 32.1.\n\ninformation.\n\nFor English the results are similar: the most confusing type of errors that leads to complete loss of meaning is word choice.\n\nIt is also difficult to describe or set rules for this type of error.\n\nOur model handles punctuation, word order mistakes and grammatical errors well.\n\nverb-object disagreement in Estonian UID19 have been corrected.\n\nDepending on the error types that have been combined our model manages quite well and corrects all or several errors present.\n\nThe results get better if the input has been automatically spell-checked.\n\nTo conclude this section, our model reliable corrects grammatical, spelling and word order errors on , with more mixed performance on lexical choice errors\n\nand some unnecessary paraphrasing of the input.\n\nThis could be fixed by either using character-based NMT or doing automatic spelling correction prior to applying our current model.\n\nThe resulting pairs\n\nAnna's bag?\n\nin the more informal.\n\nThis has given an opportunity to train new models on the shared datasets and get an objective comparison of results.\n\nThe top model of the CONLL shared task in 2014 used a combined model of rule-based approach and MT  .\n\nAll of these require annotated data or considerable effort to create, whereas our model is much more resource-independent.\n\nAnother focus of the newer research is on creating GEC models without human-annotated resources.\n\nNMT model is adapted to monolingual transfer and performs grammatical error correction and style transfer.\n\nWe experimented with three languages and presented extensive evaluation of the model on both tasks.\n\nWe used publicly available software and data and believe that our work can be easily reproduced.\n\nFuture work includes exploring adaptations of this approach to both tasks separately, while keeping the low cost of creating such models.\n\nperplexity has not improved for 32 consecutive checkpoints.\n\nThe parameters of a single best checkpoint were used for all translations, with beam size set to 5.",
